A mean gap from this synthetic case is not the published average in the original work. Researchers should not repeat a numerical result across articles as if it had come from real participants.

== Within-person, between-person and group-level claims ==

A study of social judgment can show a positive mean group gap while some participants accurately predict attention or underestimate it. An average may be dominated by unusual items, observer tasks or social settings. A repeated-measures design with multiple participants and multiple observer groups can estimate variability at both levels, for example using a hierarchical model,

<math display="block">Y_{ij}\sim\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p_{ij}),\qquad\operatorname{logit}(p_{ij})=\beta_0+u_i+\beta_s s_{ij}+\cdots,</math>

where <math>u_i</math> captures participant-level heterogeneity and <math>s_{ij}</math> could represent item salience. This is a possible statistical model, not a claim that the original published researchers used this exact specification.

Observer reports may be noisy. A “did you notice?” question can cue memory or change what counts as recognition; multiple-choice identification can have chance-level success. Researchers must separate genuine noticeability from demand or guesswork and predefine whether false positives count.

### Statistical uncertainty and design effects

If a participant interacts with 20 observers who all share the same experience, they are not 20 independent replications of the participant-level bias. Clustering reduces effective sample size relative to naïve independent-observer calculations. Randomization across stimuli, observer instructions and participant conditions is needed to isolate the influence of salience or perspective-taking interventions.

Confidence intervals should be constructed on the actual sampling hierarchy, and measurement reliability assessed for both participant predictions and observer outcomes. Different studies might use absolute counts, percentages, recognition tasks or hypothetical questions; these measures cannot be automatically pooled into one universal “spotlight effect size.”

== Contrast with related constructs ==

The illusion of transparency refers to the perceived visibility of private internal states, such as nervousness. The spotlight effect focuses on the perceived salience of externally visible attributes or actions. Both can involve egocentric estimation, but they do not have identical dependent variables or necessarily identical mechanisms.<ref name="overview"/>

Social anxiety includes clinical symptom dimensions, avoidance patterns and functional impairment, none of which is required for the spotlight effect's basic experimental definition. A person showing positive <math>d_i</math> in a laboratory task cannot be diagnosed on that basis.

The effect is also not equivalent to pluralistic ignorance, self-serving bias, confirmation bias or the halo effect. Similar-looking social judgments should be differentiated by how each is measured and its historically documented experimental paradigm.

== Alternative explanations, boundary conditions and replication ==

The spotlight effect can vary with audience familiarity, cultural conventions, participant age, group membership, event conspicuousness, actual norms and whether the observation is expected. A conspicuous violation may be accurately noticed by most observers. Real social evaluations sometimes produce serious consequences, so the finding must not be converted into advice that all public mistakes are harmless.

Replications should match the original operational definitions, pre-register exclusions, verify manipulation checks and test measurement invariance across contexts. Direct replication and conceptual extension answer different questions; if a new paradigm gives a different result, investigators should ask whether the audience, cue salience or self-focused attention was changed.

The scientific status of an effect is best expressed as evidence for a family of context-sensitive judgmental tendencies, not a universal constant. A negative result in one well-powered design may constrain the effect's scope without retroactively proving the original work was fabricated; equally, one famous demonstration is not a license to skip independent verification.

== Artificial-agent and design analogies ==

An artificial agent estimating how closely its output will be scrutinized is performing a computational prediction task. It may exhibit calibration errors if its model of reviewers is wrong. That functional analogy does not establish the same social-psychological anchoring mechanism found in human participants.

A suitable engineering experiment would measure agent predictions of observer behavior against human or automated reviewer outcomes, controlling task type and evaluation knowledge. It must not assign a human “spotlight effect” label solely because an agent produced an overconfident sentence.

Human-centered interface design can use the broader lesson that internal salience to the actor differs from external attention. But actionable design should be grounded in measured user behavior and system outcomes rather than assumed bias prevalence.

== Reproducibility requirements ==

Report recruitment, audience exposure, stimulus-selection rules, prediction timing, observer measurement protocol, analysis unit, clustering, exclusion rules, mean calibration gap and uncertainty. Distinguish self-report from independently verified observer recognition.

The synthetic fixture above should reproduce <math>\hat p=0.30</math>, gap <math>d=0.30</math>, Brier scores 0.30 and 0.21, and difference 0.09. Mathematical verification is separate from empirical effect validation.

The spotlight effect is a useful concept in social judgment, with original experimental support and important limits. Its epistemic value lies in testable discrepancy between perceived and observed noticeability—not in treating other people's attention as always negligible.

